x) whose graph can be sketched over its domain in one unbroken motion is an example of a continuous function. Such functions play an important role in the study of calculus and its applications."},{"x":0.11186,"y":0.75522,"w":0.01963,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"500"},{"x":0.29117,"y":0.76196,"w":0.01501,"h":0.01326,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Q4"},{"x":0.08751,"y":0.77628,"w":0.01461,"h":0.07899,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Distance fallen (m)"},{"x":0.27361,"y":0.78089,"w":0.01436,"h":0.01326,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Q3"},{"x":0.11186,"y":0.78123,"w":0.01963,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"375"},{"x":0.2466,"y":0.80301,"w":0.01436,"h":0.01326,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Q2"},{"x":0.11186,"y":0.8067,"w":0.01963,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"250"},{"x":0.21123,"y":0.82429,"w":0.01436,"h":0.01326,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Q1"},{"x":0.11186,"y":0.83218,"w":0.01963,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"125"},{"x":0.22353,"y":0.8648,"w":0.10103,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"5 10"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.87944,"w":0.15776,"h":0.01658,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Continuity at a Point"},{"x":0.17642,"y":0.88014,"w":0.09668,"h":0.01142,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Elapsed time (sec)"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.90117,"w":0.25888,"h":0.04729,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Figure 2.34 Connecting plotted points by an unbroken curve from experimental data Q1, Q2, Q3, c for a falling object."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.90441,"w":0.59305,"h":0.02962,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"To understand continuity, it helps to consider a function like that in Figure 2.35, whose limits we investigated in Example 2 in the last section."}]}